From: Vratislav Podzimek <vratislav.podzimek@northern.tech>
Date: Sun, 3 Feb 2019 16:53:40 +0100
Subject: Use 512bit keys in LUKS by default

This is what Debian FDE does and what was suggested in the
following research/analysis:

https://www.whonix.org/wiki/Full_Disk_Encryption_and_Encrypted_Images#Protection_Against_Powerful_Adversaries

We need to adapt the tests because AES only supports 256bit keys
and only the XTS mode splits the 512bit key into two parts.

Fixes: GH-416
(cherry picked from commit 9dc4e2463860810cac5a1dbfb7064c47200260f6)
---
 src/plugins/crypto.h |  2 +-
 tests/crypto_test.py | 26 +++++++++++++-------------
 2 files changed, 14 insertions(+), 14 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/plugins/crypto.h b/src/plugins/crypto.h
index 81a051c..8848c46 100644
--- a/src/plugins/crypto.h
+++ b/src/plugins/crypto.h
@@ -36,7 +36,7 @@ typedef enum {
 /* 20 chars * 6 bits per char (64-item charset) = 120 "bits of security" */
 #define BD_CRYPTO_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE_LENGTH 20
 
-#define DEFAULT_LUKS_KEYSIZE_BITS 256
+#define DEFAULT_LUKS_KEYSIZE_BITS 512
 #define DEFAULT_LUKS_CIPHER "aes-xts-plain64"
 #define DEFAULT_LUKS2_SECTOR_SIZE 512
 
